#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:144
#: cba8420916f44da6835fa3bf6177984a
msgid "Setting up networking for HVMs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:147
#: d345f08a61ff4a5e83fa9737ebb46234
msgid ""
"Just like standard app qubes, an HVM gets a fixed IP addresses centrally "
"assigned by Qubes. Normally, Qubes agent scripts (or services on Windows) "
"running within each app qube are responsible for setting up networking "
"within the qube according to the configuration created by Qubes (through :"
"ref:`keys <developer/debugging/vm-interface:qubesdb>` exposed by dom0 to the "
"qube). Such centrally-managed networking infrastructure allows for `advanced "
"networking configurations <https://blog.invisiblethings.org/2011/09/28/"
"playing-with-qubes-networking-for-fun.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:149
#: 68e188910ec04c859810974b07fcad42
msgid ""
"A generic HVM such as a standard Windows or Ubuntu installation, however, "
"has no Qubes agent scripts running inside it initially and thus requires "
"manual configuration of networking so that it matches the values assigned by "
"Qubes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:151
#: e9cc495f7fd749f7803fa7fae3959cf9
msgid ""
"Even though we do have a small DHCP server that runs inside the HVM’s "
"untrusted stub domain to make the manual network configuration unnecessary "
"for many qubes, this won’t work for most modern Linux distributions, which "
"contain Xen networking PV drivers (but not Qubes tools), which bypass the "
"stub-domain networking. (Their net frontends connect directly to the net "
"backend in the :term:`net qube  <net qube>`.) In this instance, our DHCP "
"server is not useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:153
#: 932f0dfbf4004314b665429393bc0a49
msgid ""
"In order to manually configure networking in a qube, one should first find "
"out the IP/netmask/gateway assigned to the particular qube by Qubes. This "
"can be seen, e.g., in the Qube Manager in the qube’s properties:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:155
#: 01be63957b1d4efdbd8cce5b771ba5e7
msgid "r4.0-manager-networking-config.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:160
#: 38e504e173f74f3283027391320b1399
msgid ""
"Alternatively, one can use the ``qvm-ls -n`` command to obtain the same "
"information (IP/netmask/gateway). The Qube Settimgs shows a netmask of "
"255.255.255.255. This is not suitable for most standalones, and you will "
"need to use a different value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:162
#: 21d45adbb1ad468dbcd1175ab6ee8845
msgid ""
"In Qubes, the IP address is usually in range 10.137.0.0/16, with disposables "
"in range 10.138.0.0/16, and DNS set to ``10.139.1.1`` and ``10.139.1.2``. "
"The simplest solution is to set the netmask to 255.0.0.0 - standard for a "
"class A network. If you want a more restricted solution you could use "
"255.252.0.0, or 255.255.255.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms.rst:164
#: 0488ff3a7b0d466b91ecf7b1b20fec45
msgid ""
"There is :ref:`opt-in support <developer/system/networking:ipv6>` for IPv6 "
"forwarding."
msgstr ""

